{"ok": true, "summary": "Root cause: the dynamic build compiled generator expressions with the same eager loop as list comprehensions (`__out.push` per item at creation), so conditions, inner iterables and the element all ran when the genexp was written instead of when the consumer pulled. Only the outermost iterable should be precomputed.

Fix (src/dynpy/emit.rs): compile `Ex::Comp` of kind 'g' to a lazy iterator value. The outermost iterable (and a `zip(...)` pair) is evaluated/iterated in the enclosing scope; the rest becomes a small state machine inside an `It::GenExp` closure that lazily creates per-level inner iterators, assigns targets, evaluates `if`s and the element on each pull, resets inner iterators on exhaustion, and carries a state counter. Free-name analysis (`expr_free`/`genexp_free`/`nested_scope_free`) marks enclosing locals that the lazy part reads as cells, and makes genexp targets cells when a nested lambda/comprehension reads them. A `yield` anywhere except the outermost iterable is now refused (CPython syntax error). Also added `It::GenExp` and `genexp_iter` in src/dynpy/rt_ops.rs so an exception escaping from the genexp exhausts it once, matching CPython (`next` then raises StopIteration).

Tested: the conformance test now matches in both normal (path dynamic) and forced dynamic builds; exact script output matches CPython. Also verified short-circuiting with side effects, later writes to captured names, inner-iterable laziness, multiple `if`s, tuple/zip unpacking, nested genexp/lambda cell capture, walrus binding to the enclosing scope, exception timing/exhaustion, and the refused `yield` case. Performance on the reviewer's benchmark (`sum(x*x for x in range(3_000_000)); max(len(str(i)) for i in range(n)); any(i < 0 for i in range(n))`): forced dynamic total ~0.55 s (sum ~0.13 s, max ~0.34 s, any ~0.08 s), faster than the previous eager build's 0.62 s (CPython ~0.35 s; typed build ~0.14 s)."}
